applyconfiguration-gen: With<Field> sets the shadowed promoted field when a direct field has the same name, so the value is lost

A struct embeds a struct inline, and also declares a direct field with the same Go name and JSON name as a promoted field. The generated `With<Field>` setter writes to the promoted field. `encoding/json` serializes only the direct (less nested) field, so the value is silently lost:

```
type Base struct {
	Enabled bool   `json:"enabled,omitempty"`
	Mode    string `json:"mode,omitempty"`
}

type Override struct {
	Base    `json:",inline"`
	Enabled *bool `json:"enabled,omitempty"`
}
```

Generated:

```
func (b *OverrideApplyConfiguration) WithEnabled(value bool) *OverrideApplyConfiguration {
	b.BaseApplyConfiguration.Enabled = &value
	return b
}
```

```
json.Marshal(v1.Override{Enabled: &t})        -> {"enabled":true}
json.Marshal(ac.Override().WithEnabled(true)) -> {}
```

The code compiles. The apply request does not contain the field.

`WithEnabled` sets the direct field `b.Enabled`, which shadows the promoted field in Go and in JSON.

Use the two types above in a `+genclient` type, as in this example:

```
type WidgetSpec struct {
	Override Override `json:"override,omitempty"`
}

// +genclient
type Widget struct {
	metav1.TypeMeta   `json:",inline"`
	metav1.ObjectMeta `json:"metadata,omitempty"`
	Spec              WidgetSpec `json:"spec,omitempty"`
}
```

```
go run k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/applyconfiguration-gen@v0.37.1 \
  --output-dir ./ac --output-pkg example.com/repro/ac ./api/v1
```

`generateWithFuncs` in `applyconfiguration.go` goes through the members in declaration order. It skips a setter whose member name it already generated. When the embedded struct comes first, the promoted setter takes the name, and the setter for the direct field is not generated. If the direct field comes first, the output is correct. A possible fix: generate the setters for the direct fields before the setters of embedded structs. This follows the Go rule that the less nested field wins.

applyconfiguration-gen: apply configurations ignore custom MarshalJSON/UnmarshalJSON of a struct type

A struct type with JSON-tagged fields and custom `MarshalJSON`/`UnmarshalJSON` methods gets a field-by-field apply configuration. The apply configuration does not have the custom methods. As a result, the apply request has a different wire format than the typed object:

```
typed object:        {"address":"a:1"}
apply configuration: {"address":{"host":"a","port":"1"}}
```

The API server sees a different value, or rejects the request. The generated code compiles, so nothing reports the problem.

A type with custom JSON serialization is an atomic value. The generator uses the Go type in the parent apply configuration (`WithAddress(value v1.Address)`) and generates no field-by-field builder. This is the same as for types that have no JSON-tagged members, for example `resource.Quantity`.

`api/v1/types.go` (with `// +groupName=example.com` in `doc.go`):

```
package v1

import (
	"encoding/json"

	metav1 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1"
)

type Address struct {
	Host string `json:"host,omitempty"`
[... 24 more lines]
```

```
go run k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/applyconfiguration-gen@v0.37.1 \
  --output-dir ./ac --output-pkg example.com/repro/ac ./api/v1
```

```
typed, _ := json.Marshal(v1.WidgetSpec{Address: v1.Address{Host: "a", Port: "1"}})
apply, _ := json.Marshal(ac.WidgetSpec().WithAddress(ac.Address().WithHost("a").WithPort("1")))
// typed: {"address":"a:1"}
// apply: {"address":{"host":"a","port":"1"}}
```

`requiresApplyConfiguration` in `refgraph.go` looks only at JSON-tagged members. A possible fix: also return false when the type, or a pointer to it, has a `MarshalJSON` or `UnmarshalJSON` method. The parent then uses the type as a whole value.
